course: dictionary
lesson: 10957
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence around them tells you which one you need. Wall is a good example. Read this: «Shout hello in a big empty hall and a moment later the walls shout it back.» Here the walls are the sides of the hall, and the echo bounces off them.
explain: The same word can mean a fence around a garden, field or town. «Never jump from high walls or roofs for a dare; falls can break bones or worse.» Here the walls are the sides of buildings or garden fences, and the falls show they are high.
explain: Wall can also mean something that stops you. In «I hit a wall in my maths homework», nobody hit a brick wall. It means you got stuck and could not go on. Always check by swapping your meaning into the sentence to see if it still makes sense.
fact: A wall is one of the sides of a room or building, or a fence of stone or brick around a garden, field or town.
fact: «A wall of water» means something huge that stops or hits you, like a great wave.
fact: «Hit a wall» means to get stuck and not be able to go on.
fact: The wall of a body part is its outer layer, like the stomach wall.
fact: «Off the wall» is a phrase that means strange or unusual, and «wall chart» means a big picture of facts hung on a wall.
exercise: «Shout hello in a big empty hall and a moment later the walls shout it back.» In this sentence, what are the walls? | A big wave in the sea | A stone path up a hill | *The sides of the hall | A fence around a garden
exercise: «Never jump from high walls or roofs for a dare.» What does wall mean here? | A chart pinned up in class | A wall of water in the sea | *A garden fence or the side of a building | The outer layer of your stomach
exercise: Kai says, «I hit a wall in my maths homework.» What does hit a wall mean? | *Got stuck and could not go on | Finished the homework very quickly | Painted a wall in bright colours | Ran into a wall by accident and bumped his head
test: «A flask has two walls with almost no air between them, so heat can hardly cross.» In this sentence, what does walls mean? | A fence around a field | Rays of heat | The outer skin of your body | *The sides of the flask
test: Maths question: «How much paint do you need for a wall?» What must you work out to answer it? | The length of the room plus its width | Its weight in kilograms | *Its area, which is length times height | The number of pictures hung on it
exam: «A submarine's strong walls resist the squeeze of deep water.» Which meaning of wall fits best here? | *The outer body of the submarine | A fence around a garden | A wall of water in the sea | A stone wall built to keep out an army
